WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.062 --> 00:00:15.034
I’m Louise Aigrain and I’m part of the R&amp;D, DNA pipelines team here at the Sanger Institute.

00:00:15.034 --> 00:00:20.022
My job is to test and develop new methods to sequence DNA.

00:00:20.022 --> 00:00:25.889
A DNA pipeline is the process of taking DNA samples

00:00:25.889 --> 00:00:31.614
preparing them for sequencing and sequencing them, before releasing the data to the researcher.

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:36.849
Because we work with DNA samples and sometimes the samples have a very low amount of DNA

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:40.836
we need to make sure that we will never contaminate them with our own DNA

00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:43.751
and that's why we wear lab coats and gloves all the time.

00:00:43.751 --> 00:00:52.428
We receive the samples in 96 well plates and they’ve already been extracted and purified from cell tissue or blood,

00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:59.770
when we receive those samples we need to check the quality of the DNA to check that is still very long strings of genomic DNA

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.353
- that's what we call the quality control or QC step.

00:01:03.353 --> 00:01:07.322
We really don't want to waste a lot of the sample for quality control

00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:15.586
that's why we are using the Mosquito robot for that step because it's able to pipette only nanolitres of DNA.

00:01:15.586 --> 00:01:22.822
These nanolitres of DNA pipetted by the robot are then mixed with a solution containing a fluorescent dye.

00:01:22.822 --> 00:01:27.494
These molecules of dye glow very, very bright only when they touch DNA

00:01:27.494 --> 00:01:34.274
so we can relate the level of fluorescence with the actual concentration of DNA in the sample.

00:01:34.274 --> 00:01:41.505
Then when we are sure that our DNA is of good quality and in good amounts, we start the library construction itself.

00:01:41.505 --> 00:01:45.826
This takes place in our pre-PCR  lab on the Bravo robots.

00:01:45.826 --> 00:01:52.500
The Bravo robots will be able to pipette some solution, containing what we call adaptors.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:59.690
These adaptors are very short fragments of DNA that get attached or ligated to the end of our DNA samples.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:05.449
The adaptors have two goals - they allow the sample to attach at the surface of the flow cell

00:02:05.449 --> 00:02:12.536
and they also contain a tag that will be part of our sequencing data and will allow us to always know

00:02:12.536 --> 00:02:16.154
which fragment of DNA came from which sample.

00:02:16.154 --> 00:02:23.019
Because we process such a high number of samples we need to make sure that we never exchange them or lose them

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:26.541
and that's why we use barcodes on each of our plates.

00:02:26.541 --> 00:02:32.110
We have a process called LIMS - which stands for Laboratory Information Management System -

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:39.143
which allows us to scan all those barcodes and track the samples all along the library preparation process.

00:02:39.143 --> 00:02:47.714
After attaching the adaptors to our DNA fragment, we take the samples off the robot deck onto a bench.

00:02:47.714 --> 00:02:54.390
Because we are sharing those benches with a lot of people in the lab they need to remain very tidy and clean,

00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:00.223
so we have divided the space with tape and labels so that we know exactly where things are.

00:03:00.223 --> 00:03:08.477
The samples are now ready for PCR amplification and during this process we have to heat the sample to a high temperature.

00:03:08.477 --> 00:03:13.557
To avoid evaporation we seal the plates with a very tight seal.

00:03:13.557 --> 00:03:19.251
PCR amplification has to be done in a different lab to avoid cross contamination

00:03:19.251 --> 00:03:23.894
and we pass the sample through a special hatch between those two labs.

00:03:23.894 --> 00:03:30.798
Each lab has different colours of lab coats to make sure that we are not contaminating samples between the labs.

00:03:30.798 --> 00:03:36.731
We then take the samples and put them in a PCR machine that is able to amplify the DNA.

00:03:36.731 --> 00:03:44.223
The DNA samples are now ready for sequencing and in order to be loaded on the sequencer, we need to remove the seal.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:49.521
Now that's quite a tricky step, despite the fact that it looks very simple and no robot can do it.

00:03:49.521 --> 00:03:56.267
It has to be done by hand to avoid splashes between the samples, which would lead to cross contamination.

00:03:56.267 --> 00:04:03.549
The samples are then put on a flow cell, loaded onto the sequencer and the sequencing run can start.

00:04:03.549 --> 00:04:10.344
During a run we can check on the monitor of each sequencer, the quality of the data being created

00:04:10.344 --> 00:04:13.479
and check that everything is working fine on the sequencer.

00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:21.138
When a sequencing run is finished the data is transferred to a data centre,which is a huge cluster of computers.

00:04:21.138 --> 00:04:27.473
The last stage for our pipelines team is to check that we have produced enough data and of good quality

00:04:27.473 --> 00:04:36.382
and when that last check is done, the researchers that initially requested the sequencing are able to access the data in the cluster.

00:04:36.382 --> 00:04:42.360
I really like working in the DNA pipelines 
because it uses a lot of different skills.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:48.648
I studied chemistry and then I worked in a biophysics lab, so that's why I particularly like it.

00:04:48.648 --> 00:04:51.347
It's always exciting and never boring.

